{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B-core/r1-correctness.json\"","aggregated_output":"{\"type\":\"result\",\"subtype\":\"success\",\"is_error\":false,\"api_error_status\":null,\"duration_ms\":881612,\"duration_api_ms\":882474,\"num_turns\":15,\"result\":\"Now I have all the information needed. Let me produce the structured review.\\n\\n{\\\"review_id\\\":\\\"r2-correctness\\\",\\\"slice\\\":\\\"WP-SCP-024/024B-core\\\",\\\"lens\\\":\\\"correctness\\\",\\\"reviewer\\\":\\\"sonnet-r2-correctness\\\",\\\"date\\\":\\\"2026-05-09\\\",\\\"verdict\\\":\\\"CHANGES_REQUESTED\\\",\\\"summary\\\":\\\"All four R1 findings (CORR-CRIT-001 two-pass bypass, CORR-MAJ-001 unreachable not-applicable branch, CORR-MAJ-002 run_enable_case residual, CORR-MAJ-003 missing bypass test) have been resolved. The single-pass re.findall regex eliminates the indented-not-applicable bypass; the not-applicable case branch is now reachable via ([^\\\\\\\\r\\\\\\\\n]+?); the enable-required-check.sh reference and run_enable_case helper are gone; case 20 covers the indented-not-applicable-before-onboarded scenario. The slice correctly implements bootstrap-only guard, 40-char lowercase hex SHA validation, system-path denylist with macOS /private/* canonicalization via readlink -f, 4-file emission with SHA256 MANIFEST audit, template with Renovate marker immediately before uses: line, least-privilege permissions (contents:read + statuses:write only), fork-PR refusal if: clause, five-path cascade-status state machine with exactly-one-match assertion, adopter-slug canonicalization, SAFE-003 log-entry exactly-one assertion, D-044 correctly dated and scoped, STATUS.md 2026-05-09 PM chain entry present, and plan-doc §6 split-amendment with FUP-ACC-INSTALL-TARGET-REPO-001 gate preserved. Two MAJ findings remain: the YAML lint check's pyyaml-absent handler exits code 1 under set -euo pipefail (turns a skip into a hard abort), and the --pr mode test lacks onboarded-operator-bump and blocked-on-adopter-conflict coverage (the blocked-on-adopter-conflict negative log-modification assertion traverses gh pr diff --name-only, a code path never exercised by run_pr_case). Two MIN findings: --diff-base path lacks error handling for invalid DIFF_BASE (inconsistency with --pr path's 2>/dev/null || true), and the 024B-core DISPATCH-NOTE uses backtick-prose form for cascade-status rather than the bare line-start format that check-invocation-log-entry.sh requires.\\\",\\\"findings\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"CORR-MAJ-001-R2\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MAJ\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"test_scaffold_downstream.sh YAML lint check handles pyyaml ImportError via raise SystemExit(str), which Python resolves to exit code 1. set -euo pipefail then aborts the entire test runner, turning an intended graceful skip into a hard FAIL that masks all subsequent test cases.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"tests/scaffolder/test_scaffold_downstream.sh:132-136 — except ImportError as exc: raise SystemExit('SKIP: pyyaml not installed — install it to run YAML lint checks') from exc. Python 3: SystemExit(non-int) exits code 1. set -euo pipefail at line 2 causes the script to abort on the non-zero python3 exit.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"Any CI or dev environment without pyyaml installed sees the entire scaffolder test suite report FAIL rather than a targeted SKIP. Tests after line 138 (arg-validation, CI-guard, denylist, non-HEAD-warn, default-branch variants) are never reached, producing a false-negative that masks real regressions.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Replace raise SystemExit('SKIP: ...') from exc with: print('SKIP: pyyaml not installed — install it to run YAML lint checks', file=sys.stderr); sys.exit(0). This preserves the informational message while correctly exiting 0 so set -e does not abort the runner.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"CORR-MAJ-002-R2\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MAJ\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"The --pr mode test (run_pr_case) is exercised only for cascade-status=onboarded (case16 happy path, case17 SAFE-003 phantom). No run_pr_case test exists for onboarded-operator-bump or blocked-on-adopter-conflict. The blocked-on-adopter-conflict negative log-modification assertion uses changed_files from gh pr diff --name-only — a distinct code path from git diff --name-only — and is entirely untested in PR mode.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"tests/check_invocation_log/test_check_invocation_log_entry.sh — grep of run_pr_case shows calls only at case16 (line ~552) and case17 (line ~609). check-invocation-log-entry.sh:168 — changed_files via gh pr diff; line 171 — via git diff. The branch_log_modified=0 assertion for blocked-on-adopter-conflict is tested only via --diff-base (case10).\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"A regression in the gh pr diff --name-only parsing path (e.g., unexpected path prefix, trailing whitespace, or gh CLI version difference) could cause blocked-on-adopter-conflict slices that DID modify branch-protection-log.md to pass PR-mode CI enforcement. The negative safety assertion is the core of invariant-2's blocked-on-adopter-conflict guarantee.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Add run_pr_case test for blocked-on-adopter-conflict where fake gh --name-only returns branch-protection-log.md path; expect exit 1 with 'ERROR: branch-protection-log.md was modified'. Add run_pr_case for onboarded-operator-bump where fake gh --name-only returns the log file path and --patch returns the log entry; verify STATUS.md TF-row grep still exercises local-file path (unchanged) and expect exit 0.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"CORR-MIN-001-R2\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MIN\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"In --diff-base mode, git diff at line 171 has no error guard, while the --pr path at line 169 uses 2>/dev/null || true. An invalid DIFF_BASE ref causes set -euo pipefail to abort at line 171 with a raw git error rather than a script-controlled diagnostic, and no test covers this failure mode.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"scripts/check-invocation-log-entry.sh:171 — branch_log_patch=$(git diff --unified=0 ${DIFF_BASE}..HEAD -- $BRANCH_PROTECTION_LOG_REPO_REL) — no error guard. Line 169 — || true present. test_check_invocation_log_entry.sh — no test passes an invalid --diff-base value.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"Operators running the script manually with an incorrect base ref (e.g. a typo or a force-pushed-away ref) receive an opaque git error with no context about which argument was invalid, making diagnosis harder. The asymmetry also obscures a latent failure mode where gh fails silently in --pr mode while --diff-base fails loudly.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Wrap line 171: branch_log_patch=$(git diff --unified=0 \\\\\\\"${DIFF_BASE}..HEAD\\\\\\\" -- \\\\\\\"$BRANCH_PROTECTION_LOG_REPO_REL\\\\\\\") || die \\\\\\\"ERROR: could not compute diff for diff-base '${DIFF_BASE}'; is this a valid git ref?\\\\\\\" 2. Add a test that passes an invalid --diff-base value and expects exit 2.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"CORR-MIN-002-R2\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MIN\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"The 024B-core DISPATCH-NOTE records cascade-status in backtick-prose form (`cascade-status: not applicable` — line 9) rather than as a bare line-start field. check-invocation-log-entry.sh requires ^cascade-status:\\\\\\\\s+ at column 0 (re.M). If the CI enforcement workflow (landing in 024B-extras) is ever applied repo-wide or to historical DISPATCHNOTEs, 024B-core's DISPATCH-NOTE would produce 'expected exactly one cascade-status match, found 0' and fail.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B-core/DISPATCH-NOTE.md:9 — `cascade-status: not applicable` — this slice... (backtick-wrapped, embedded in prose). check-invocation-log-entry.sh:127 — re.findall(r'^cascade-status:\\\\\\\\s+...', text, re.M) requires field at column 0. test case15b shows the correct machine-parseable form: cascade-status: not applicable as a bare line.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"No runtime defect for 024B-core because the CI enforcement workflow is deferred to 024B-extras. However, the format inconsistency mismatches what future tooling-slice DISPATCHNOTEs should use, and any repo-wide integrity sweep using check-invocation-log-entry.sh against the 024B-core DISPATCH-NOTE would produce a false CI failure.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Add a bare cascade-status: not applicable line at the top of the DISPATCH-NOTE header block (the existing backtick-prose sentence can remain as prose context). This makes the DISPATCH-NOTE self-consistent with the enforcement contract and safe for future repo-wide sweeps.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"CORR-nit-001-R2\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"nit\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"validate_manifest helper in test_scaffold_downstream.sh hardcodes expected adopter_repo='jrnb2024/test-adopter' and scorecard_emit=False. The helper is called for the happy-path and non-HEAD-SHA cases but not for the master or scorecard-emit=true variants. If a future test case passes different values and reuses validate_manifest, the hardcoded assertions will unexpectedly fail.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"tests/scaffolder/test_scaffold_downstream.sh:61-67 — if payload['adopter_repo'] != 'jrnb2024/test-adopter': raise SystemExit('unexpected adopter_repo'); if payload['scorecard_emit'] is not False: raise SystemExit. Lines 139, 182 — validate_manifest called for main happy path and non-HEAD-SHA variant only; master/develop/scorecard-emit=true variants do not call validate_manifest.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"No current defect. Brittle for future test expansion — adding a new call with different fixture values would require modifying validate_manifest rather than just calling it.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Parameterise validate_manifest to accept expected_adopter_repo and expected_scorecard_emit arguments rather than hardcoding. Update call sites to pass the current fixture values explicitly.\\\"}]}\",\"stop_reason\":\"end_turn\",\"session_id\":\"cdb14333-9eb7-4b96-91ce-f629aa722c40\",\"total_cost_usd\":1.4142677,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":9,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":132498,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":409594,\"output_tokens\":52794,\"server_tool_use\":{\"web_search_requests\":0,\"web_fetch_requests\":0},\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":132498,\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0},\"inference_geo\":\"\",\"iterations\":[{\"input_tokens\":1,\"output_tokens\":16627,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":136704,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":3175,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":3175},\"type\":\"message\"}],\"speed\":\"standard\"},\"modelUsage\":{\"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001\":{\"inputTokens\":2445,\"outputTokens\":28,\"cacheReadInputTokens\":0,\"cacheCreationInputTokens\":0,\"webSearchRequests\":0,\"costUSD\":0.002585,\"contextWindow\":200000,\"maxOutputTokens\":32000},\"claude-sonnet-4-6\":{\"inputTokens\":9,\"outputTokens\":52794,\"cacheReadInputTokens\":409594,\"cacheCreationInputTokens\":132498,\"webSearchRequests\":0,\"costUSD\":1.4116827,\"contextWindow\":200000,\"maxOutputTokens\":32000}},\"permission_denials\":[],\"terminal_reason\":\"completed\",\"fast_mode_state\":\"off\",\"uuid\":\"2f476711-ee4e-453f-bafa-a1398d769e91\"}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B-core/r1-safety_bypass.json\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B-core/r1-safety_bypass.json\"","aggregated_output":"{\"type\":\"result\",\"subtype\":\"success\",\"is_error\":false,\"api_error_status\":null,\"duration_ms\":760695,\"duration_api_ms\":761812,\"num_turns\":14,\"result\":\"I now have sufficient evidence to produce the structured findings report.\\n\\n{\\\"lens\\\":\\\"SAFETY_BYPASS\\\",\\\"slice\\\":\\\"024B-core\\\",\\\"reviewer\\\":\\\"sonnet-r1-safety_bypass\\\",\\\"round\\\":1,\\\"verdict\\\":\\\"APPROVED_WITH_FINDINGS\\\",\\\"summary\\\":\\\"The 024B-core slice ships three safety-relevant components. Verified correct under the SAFETY_BYPASS lens: the fail-closed default for absent/unrecognised cascade-status routes empty command-substitution output through the `*)` die branch — Python failure yields empty `cascade_status`, which does not match `\\\\\\\"not applicable\\\\\\\"`, so FAIL-CLOSED is preserved. The exactly-one-match assertion on cascade-status (Python `re.findall`, exits 1 on ≠1 match) and on log-entry target (`parse_entry_repo`, exits 1 on ≠1 match) correctly resist multi-occurrence and phantom-target injection respectively. Path normalization via `readlink -f`/`os.path.realpath` precedes denylist evaluation; macOS `/var/log` → `/private/var/log` canonicalization is Darwin-gated and tested. Bootstrap-only guard (exit 1) fires at line 48-50, before the SHA non-existence check (exit 2, line 148-151). Wrapper template permissions are `contents: read` + `statuses: write` only — no `id-token: write` or `attestations: write` — and the fork-PR `if:` clause is present and tested. Adopter-slug canonicalization correctly converts `.` and `_` to `-`; `re.escape(slug)` defends against regex injection; case 4h verifies `jrnb2024/repo.foo_bar` → `jrnb2024-repo-foo-bar`. Worked-example matrix ex1+ex2 pass; ex3-ex6 verified to fail in test cases 4c-4f. Two MAJ findings identified: (1) `cascade-status: not applicable` exits 0 with zero enforcement — no technical constraint prevents cohort cascade slices 024C-F from declaring it; (2) `branch_log_modified` detection uses exact-path grep against `gh pr diff --name-only` output, which is unspecified for renamed files, creating a `blocked-on-adopter-conflict` negative-assertion bypass via log-file rename. Two MIN and two nit findings complete the set. No CRIT found.\\\",\\\"findings\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"SAFE-MAJ-001\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MAJ\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"`cascade-status: not applicable` exits 0 with NO enforcement checks. The script imposes no technical constraint preventing a cohort cascade slice (024C-F) from declaring this value. All log-entry, target-match, and TF-reference checks are bypassed; CI exits 0 with a success-class message, leaving the invocation-log audit trail unverified.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"check-invocation-log-entry.sh:140-143 — `\\\\\\\"not applicable\\\\\\\"` branch prints `OK: ... not a cohort cascade slice — nothing to enforce` and exits 0 before `target_repo` extraction, `branch_log_modified` check, or TF-reference validation fire. Test case 15b confirms exit 0 even when STATUS.md and DISPATCH-NOTE contain other cascade-status artefacts. No test, schema, or runtime check prevents 024C-F DISPATCH-NOTEs from using this value.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"A 024C-F DISPATCH-NOTE declaring `cascade-status: not applicable` (e.g. copied from 024B-core template without updating) passes CI exit 0. The invocation-log entry is never required, the target repo is never verified, and the TF-reference check never fires. The primary audit trail for `enable-required-check.sh` invocations can be silently omitted; the only guard is single-operator self-review (D-031).\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Require a second discriminating declaration before accepting `not applicable`: e.g. require DISPATCH-NOTE to also contain a `slice-type: tooling` header line, or maintain an allowlist of tooling-slice IDs against which `not applicable` is permitted. This adds a second declaration that code review can verify and that cannot be accidentally copied.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"SAFE-MAJ-002\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MAJ\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"In `--pr` mode, `branch_log_modified` is set via `grep -Fxq \\\\\\\"$BRANCH_PROTECTION_LOG_REPO_REL\\\\\\\" <<<\\\\\\\"$changed_files\\\\\\\"` (exact original-path match). A PR that renames the branch-protection-log file may produce `gh pr diff --name-only` output that includes only the new path, leaving `branch_log_modified=0` despite log content being moved or added under the new path.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"check-invocation-log-entry.sh:174-177 — detection is a single exact-path grep with no rename-detection guard. `gh pr diff --name-only` rename behaviour is unspecified; GitHub rename detection may emit only the new path. `--diff-base` mode (`git diff --name-only`) similarly has no explicit `--no-renames` guard. No test case covers the rename scenario.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"A `blocked-on-adopter-conflict` PR that renames `branch-protection-log.md` (moving new log content to the renamed file) passes the `branch_log_modified==0` negative assertion. An undisclosed `enable-required-check.sh` invocation record exists under the new path while CI asserts no log modification occurred, defeating the core R3-safety property (bypass via concurrent invocation + conflict-declaration). CODEOWNERS protection constrains but does not technically prevent the attack.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Enumerate both old and new paths for renames: use `git diff --diff-filter=ACDMRT --name-only` (listing both paths) or explicitly check for any path matching `docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/*.md` in the changed-files list. Alternatively, compare the log file's content hash at DIFF_BASE and HEAD rather than relying solely on path-presence detection.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"SAFE-MIN-001\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MIN\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"`canonicalize_adopter_slug` collapses `.`, `_`, and `-` to `-`, producing identical slugs for distinct adopter repos (e.g. `owner/repo.foo`, `owner/repo_foo`, `owner/repo-foo` all yield `owner-repo-foo`). A PR for a new adopter whose canonical slug collides with a previously-onboarded adopter would accept the existing TF row, bypassing the `onboarded-operator-bump` or `blocked-on-adopter-conflict` checks.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"check-invocation-log-entry.sh:93-99 — `re.sub(r\\\\\\\"[^a-z0-9-]+\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"-\\\\\\\", s.replace(\\\\\\\"/\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"-\\\\\\\"))` merges all non-alphanumeric/hyphen chars to `-`. Plan-doc §2 states \\\\\\\"Plan-doc spec ensures unique-per-cohort\\\\\\\" as a documentation constraint only. Current 5-cohort (pim, recommender, shopify-app, mapp-doc-agent, control-tower) produces no collisions; exploitable only with future adopters.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"For the current 5-cohort, no collision is possible. For future estate expansion, a prior onboarded adopter's `TF-024X-renovate-owner-repo-foo` row satisfies the `onboarded-operator-bump` STATUS.md check for a new adopter `owner/repo.foo` that produces the same canonical slug, allowing the new adopter to close without a distinct TF row. The cross-adopter false-positive passes CI silently.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Document the slug-collision surface explicitly in D-044's trust-model claims. Optionally add a uniqueness assertion at adoption time: reject any new adopter whose canonical slug already appears in existing TF rows in branch-protection-log or STATUS.md.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"SAFE-MIN-002\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MIN\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"`--dispatch-note` and `--status-md` paths are normalized with `os.path.normpath` (resolves `.`/`..` but NOT symlinks), while `--branch-protection-log` receives full `os.path.realpath` resolution plus an explicit repo-root containment check. A symlink passed as `--dispatch-note` is silently followed to an out-of-repo target.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"check-invocation-log-entry.sh:89-103 — `normalize_path()` uses `os.path.normpath`; `BRANCH_PROTECTION_LOG_REAL` uses `os.path.realpath` with `case \\\\\\\"$BRANCH_PROTECTION_LOG_REAL\\\\\\\" in \\\\\\\"$REPO_ROOT\\\\\\\"|\\\\\\\"$REPO_ROOT\\\\\\\"/*) ;;`. No equivalent containment check for `DISPATCH_NOTE` or `STATUS_MD`. The asymmetry is structural, not incidental.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"An operator passing `--status-md /symlink/to/external/file` where the external file contains a line matching the TF-024X regex would produce a false-positive pass for `onboarded-operator-bump`. The cascade-status regex is unlikely to match arbitrary files (fail-closed in practice), but the inconsistent treatment is a hardening gap and a trust-model inaccuracy in D-044's claim that path inputs are repo-contained.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Apply `os.path.realpath` + repo-root containment check to `--dispatch-note` and `--status-md` consistently with `--branch-protection-log`. The existing `REPO_ROOT` realpath computation (line 105-109) can be reused.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"SAFE-nit-001\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"nit\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"`parse_entry_repo` branch segment character class `[A-Za-z0-9._./-]` contains redundant duplicate characters: `.` and `_` each appear twice.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"check-invocation-log-entry.sh:185 — `r\\\\\\\"^###\\\\\\\\s+.+\\\\\\\\s+—\\\\\\\\s+([A-Za-z0-9._-]+(?:/[A-Za-z0-9._-]+)?)@([A-Za-z0-9._./-]+)$\\\\\\\"` — the branch capture group `[A-Za-z0-9._./-]` has two `.` and two `_`.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"No security impact; the character class matches correctly. Purely cosmetic.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Replace `[A-Za-z0-9._./-]` with `[A-Za-z0-9._/-]`.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"SAFE-nit-002\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"nit\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"Test harness covers two DIFFERENT cascade-status values (case 15) and one indented+unindented same-value pair (case 14), but does not explicitly test two identical unindented cascade-status values in a single DISPATCH-NOTE.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"tests/check_invocation_log/test_check_invocation_log_entry.sh:437-473 — cases 14 and 15 cover adjacent terrain. Code analysis confirms FAIL-CLOSED behavior (Python `re.findall` returns 2 matches → exits 1 → `cascade_status=\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"` → `*)` die), but no test explicitly exercises this exact path.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"No vulnerability; code is fail-closed in this scenario. Test coverage gap only.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Add a case with two identical unindented `cascade-status: onboarded` lines; assert non-zero exit with stderr containing `expected exactly one cascade-status match, found 2`.\\\"}]}\",\"stop_reason\":\"end_turn\",\"session_id\":\"cf226841-1403-432d-88a9-2fd884cb1cf7\",\"total_cost_usd\":1.1671684,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":6,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":114174,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":177313,\"output_tokens\":45586,\"server_tool_use\":{\"web_search_requests\":0,\"web_fetch_requests\":0},\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":114174,\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0},\"inference_geo\":\"\",\"iterations\":[{\"input_tokens\":1,\"output_tokens\":2971,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":77361,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":44194,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":44194},\"type\":\"message\"}],\"speed\":\"standard\"},\"modelUsage\":{\"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001\":{\"inputTokens\":1879,\"outputTokens\":27,\"cacheReadInputTokens\":0,\"cacheCreationInputTokens\":0,\"webSearchRequests\":0,\"costUSD\":0.002014,\"contextWindow\":200000,\"maxOutputTokens\":32000},\"claude-sonnet-4-6\":{\"inputTokens\":6,\"outputTokens\":45586,\"cacheReadInputTokens\":177313,\"cacheCreationInputTokens\":114174,\"webSearchRequests\":0,\"costUSD\":1.1651544,\"contextWindow\":200000,\"maxOutputTokens\":32000}},\"permission_denials\":[],\"terminal_reason\":\"completed\",\"fast_mode_state\":\"off\",\"uuid\":\"fdb7b7d4-4d35-4b1b-b3e8-323829487f8e\"}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B-core/r1-completeness_governance.json\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B-core/r1-completeness_governance.json\"","aggregated_output":"{\"type\":\"result\",\"subtype\":\"success\",\"is_error\":false,\"api_error_status\":null,\"duration_ms\":450447,\"duration_api_ms\":451094,\"num_turns\":12,\"result\":\"{\\\"lens\\\":\\\"COMPLETENESS_GOVERNANCE\\\",\\\"verdict\\\":\\\"CHANGES_REQUESTED\\\",\\\"summary\\\":\\\"024B-core scope split is well-governed: SCOPE-CORRECTION-2026-05-09.md carries empirical R6→R7→R8 trajectory, an explicit why-not-descope section, and a carry-forward reference. The D-044 decision row cites the scope-correction doc, is scoped to core deliverables, and is filed ACCEPTED. STATUS.md split-decision entry documents the extras-parking branch and three outcome rows. The plan-doc §6 amendment gates 024C on both halves plus FUP-ACC-INSTALL-TARGET-REPO-001 (captured in the split note). VERSIONING.md alignment is clean — scaffolder, template, and CI script are internal tooling, no version bump required. Anti-descoping posture is sound; no extras-scope content leaked into core files. Code coverage of plan-doc invariant 2 is complete across all five cascade-status check-paths including the fail-closed default and adopter-slug canonicalisation. One MAJ is filed: the DISPATCH-NOTE declares cascade-status: not applicable inside a backtick markdown span (line starts with the backtick delimiter, not cascade-status:), so the enforcement script's Python regex ^cascade-status:\\\\\\\\s+ finds zero matches and would exit with an error if run against this file. This breaks machine-parseability of the DISPATCH-NOTE per plan-doc invariant 2 and will cause CI self-dogfood failure when 024B-extras wires the enforcement workflow. Four lower-severity findings cover the scorecard-emit true permissions gap in the template, asymmetric negative-case test coverage for the blocked-on-adopter-conflict regex path, missing manifest validation for the scorecard-emit true variant, and a stale invocation-log convention claim in the DECISIONS.md reservation header note.\\\",\\\"findings\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"CG-R1-MAJ-001\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MAJ\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"DISPATCH-NOTE cascade-status field is backtick-wrapped so the enforcement script regex cannot parse it, finding 0 matches instead of 1.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"DISPATCH-NOTE.md line 9: starts with backtick character — `cascade-status: not applicable` — not with cascade-status:. Python regex ^cascade-status:\\\\\\\\s+([^\\\\\\\\r\\\\\\\\n]+?)\\\\\\\\s*$ with re.M anchors to line-start and finds 0 matches, triggering the exactly-one-match error exit. test_check_invocation_log_entry.sh case 15b uses a bare cascade-status: not applicable at line start as the passing fixture, confirming required format.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"When 024B-extras ships the CI enforcement workflow, any PR that checks the 024B-core DISPATCH-NOTE exits non-zero. Also establishes a formatting precedent that would silently break cascade-slice self-dogfood if copied.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Add a bare cascade-status: not applicable line at the start of a line in the DISPATCH-NOTE (no backtick prefix), replacing or preceding the backtick-formatted inline-code span, so the regex finds exactly one match.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"CG-R1-MIN-001\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MIN\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"Template adopter-wrapper.yml.tmpl has no inline comment warning that scorecard-emit: true requires additional permissions not present in the template.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"Template lines 9-12: permissions block emits only contents: read + statuses: write regardless of {{SCORECARD_EMIT}}. TF-023E-002 documents that attestations: write + id-token: write are required for the attest-scorecard job when scorecard-emit: true. DISPATCH-NOTE notes 'opt-in adopters add attestations:write + id-token:write per ADOPT-001 §12.7.15 step 2' but the template file itself is silent.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"Operator using --scorecard-emit true receives a syntactically valid but functionally broken wrapper; adopter CI fails at startup_failure with no in-file indication of the root cause. Failure occurs silently at scaffolder run time.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Add an inline comment in the template adjacent to the permissions block noting that scorecard-emit: true additionally requires attestations: write + id-token: write per ADOPT-001 §12.7.15 step 2 and TF-023E-002 before enabling.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"CG-R1-MIN-002\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MIN\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"test_check_invocation_log_entry.sh negative TF-format cases (ex3–ex6) are only exercised on the onboarded-operator-bump path; blocked-on-adopter-conflict path has no parallel negative-format tests.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"Cases 4c–4f test bare-prefix, empty-description, whitespace, and too-short TF formats via STATUS.md for the operator-bump path. Case 11 tests missing TF reference for the conflict path but includes no format-validation negatives. DISPATCH-NOTE AC claims '6 worked-example matrix cases from plan-doc §2 invariant 2 (2 ✅ + 4 ❌)' covered, but the 4 ❌ cases test only the operator-bump path.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"Shared regex means logic is correct; however test asymmetry leaves conflict-path TF-format enforcement unproven. A future regex regression specific to the conflict path would not be caught by the test suite.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Add cases mirroring 4c–4f for the blocked-on-adopter-conflict path: bare-prefix stub, empty description, all-whitespace description, and too-short description in the DISPATCH-NOTE TF conflict reference.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"CG-R1-MIN-003\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MIN\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"validate_manifest in test_scaffold_downstream.sh only asserts scorecard_emit is False; the --scorecard-emit true variant has no manifest validation.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"test_scaffold_downstream.sh lines 67-68: if payload['scorecard_emit'] is not False: raise SystemExit. The master-variant test (lines 186-197 using --scorecard-emit true) only checks wrapper file content and does not call validate_manifest. The manifest's scorecard_emit boolean field is never asserted for the true case.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"A regression that emits scorecard_emit: false in the manifest when --scorecard-emit true is passed would go undetected by the test suite.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Parameterise validate_manifest to accept an expected_scorecard_emit argument and call it in the scorecard-emit true variant test block with expected value True.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"CG-R1-MIN-004\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MIN\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"DECISIONS.md reservation header note for D-044 still references 'scaffolder writes a rendered-template log entry alongside enable-required-check.sh's branch-protection log', which was not implemented.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"DECISIONS.md lines 29-46: reservation note retains 'Invocation-log convention extension: scaffolder writes a rendered-template log entry alongside enable-required-check.sh's branch-protection log.' Filed D-044 row describes actual implementation as MANIFEST.json SHA256 audit to output dir only. Plan-doc §5.3 also specifies only MANIFEST.json.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"Operators reading the reservation note before the filed D-044 row expect a scaffolder-to-branch-protection-log write path that does not exist; could cause confusion at 024B-extras authoring or future adopter onboarding time.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Update the D-044 reservation header note to replace the stale invocation-log-extension language with the actual implementation: MANIFEST.json SHA256 audit emitted to output directory.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"CG-R1-nit-001\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"nit\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"Plan-doc §6 024C table row acceptance criteria does not inline the 024B-extras and FUP-ACC-INSTALL-TARGET-REPO-001 gate conditions.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"docs/plans/WP-SCP-024-estate-cascade.md §6 024C row AC: 'PIM policy-check / scp/policy-check required-check live; ≥1 Renovate-bump-propagation cycle merged clean; D-045 filed.' Split note at line 213 captures both prerequisites but the 024C row itself does not enumerate them, requiring cross-referencing two locations.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"A future operator reading only the 024C row risks missing the FUP-ACC-INSTALL-TARGET-REPO-001 gate; cohort onboarding could start before ACC install precondition is closed.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Add a prerequisites note to the 024C AC column: 'Prerequisites: 024B-core + 024B-extras merged + FUP-ACC-INSTALL-TARGET-REPO-001 closed (per split note).'\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"CG-R1-nit-002\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"nit\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"DISPATCH-NOTE Status line is stale: reads 'DRAFT v0.1 — awaiting Codex T3 surgical-clean dispatch' although Phase 1 Codex work is already complete.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"DISPATCH-NOTE.md final line: **Status:** DRAFT v0.1 — awaiting Codex T3 surgical-clean dispatch. DISPATCH-NOTE §Sequencing Phase 0 is marked 'done'; Phase 1 Codex work appears to have landed given the slice files exist. Status was not updated after Phase 1 completed.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"Cosmetic; reviewer sees a stale phase label and must infer actual progress from elsewhere.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Update Status line to reflect review-in-progress state, e.g. 'R1 review in progress (2026-05-09)'.\\\"}]}\",\"stop_reason\":\"end_turn\",\"session_id\":\"a11a42f4-6dca-4c5b-b0f8-42dc6bc3a28e\",\"total_cost_usd\":0.8611840499999999,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":5607,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":97383,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":146456,\"output_tokens\":28909,\"server_tool_use\":{\"web_search_requests\":0,\"web_fetch_requests\":0},\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":97383,\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0},\"inference_geo\":\"\",\"iterations\":[{\"input_tokens\":1,\"output_tokens\":28038,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":